# Frontend build dependencies and artefacts.
#
# `web/dist` is produced by Vite and copied into `internal/api/webdist/` by the
# Dockerfile. Tracking it would put a regenerated binary in history on every
# build, whose diff teaches nothing.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/

# Secrets for THIS instance: `.env` holds the admin password and the
# credentials encryption key. `.env.example` is the template without real
# values, and stays tracked. `.env.local` is for machine-only tokens (e.g. GitHub PAT).
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Database backups. A meshcraft dump is the WORST file to commit: it contains
# the sealed fleet credentials AND, while MESHCRAFT_SECRET_KEY is unset, the
# key that opens them — both in the same file, which defeats encryption.
# Ignored before the first dump even exists, because a secret committed by
# mistake remains in history after deletion: the only fix is to treat it as
# leaked and rotate everything it held.
.backups/
*.sql.gz
*.dump

# NOTE — `internal/api/webdist/index.html` is NOT ignored, and that is not an
# oversight: it is a placeholder without which `go:embed all:webdist` refuses
# to compile outside Docker. The build replaces it in its own context, never
# on the host.

# Editor and filesystem traces.
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
.cursor/
# Agent instructions and working notes: kept on the maintainer's machine,
# not published.
.claude/
CLAUDE.md
TODO.md
docs/fr/06-Décisions/Reprise — *.md
scripts/englishify_drivers_batch.py
scripts/gen_driver_aliases.py
web/scripts/translate_locales.py
web/scripts/i18n_metrics.py
.idea/
.vscode/

# graphify knowledge graph: ~30 MB regenerated on demand from code and the
# vault, including a 5 MB graph.html. Derived artefact, never a source —
# committing it would put a binary that reshuffles on every touched file,
# whose diff teaches nothing.
graphify-out/

# Local build artefacts / scratch
/controller
*.log

# Compiled lab exploration tools (tools/lab*).
#
# Their SOURCE is tracked on purpose — `go build ./...` covers it, which is what
# keeps them compiling against the same x/crypto/ssh and internal/gnmipoll the
# product uses, so a capture taken with them is taken over the code path the
# product takes. The BINARIES are not: they are 3 to 20 MB each, they are rebuilt
# from source in one command, and a committed binary teaches a reader nothing
# while making every diff that touches them unreadable.
tools/labssh/labssh
tools/labfwd/labfwd
tools/labgnmi/labgnmi
tools/labattach/labattach
